---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Code from '../components/Code.astro'
import { href } from '../lib/paths'

const install = `
curl -fsSL https://sparshlabs.com/ownci/install.sh | sh
`

const workflow = `
jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: [ownci]            # the opt-in label OwnCI registers
    container: ubuntu:24.04     # every job runs in a container
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - run: make test
`
---

<BaseLayout
  title="OwnCI · Your PR. Your Compute."
  description="OwnCI turns the Linux machine you already code on into the GitHub Actions runner for your repository: set up, start, stop and remove it with one command each, with every job in a Docker container."
>
  <main id="main" class="content-page">
    <section class="hero">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-dot"></span> Open source · MVP for Linux &amp; WSL2</p>
      <h1>Your PR.<br /><span>Your Compute.</span></h1>
      <p class="lead">
        OwnCI turns the machine you already code on into the GitHub Actions runner for your
        repository. GitHub still queues the jobs and shows the check on your pull request. Your
        machine does the work, and every job runs inside a Docker container.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button-primary" href={href('/docs/')}>Get started <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        <a class="button-secondary" href={href('/how-it-works/')}>See how it works</a>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-install">
        <Code label="Install · Linux and WSL2" code={install} />
      </div>

      <div class="run-demo" role="img" aria-label="Illustration: a pull request check is queued on GitHub, runs on your machine in a container, and passes; the workspace is then wiped.">
        <div class="pr-card" aria-hidden="true">
          <div class="pr-head">
            <span class="pr-kicker">ACME/APP · PULL REQUEST</span>
            <h2>Retry failed uploads <span>#42</span></h2>
          </div>
          <div class="pr-body">
            <span class="pr-label">CHECKS</span>
            <div class="check-row">
              <div class="check-name">
                <strong>CI / build</strong>
                <small>runs-on: [ownci] · container: node:22</small>
              </div>
              <div class="check-states">
                <span class="check-state state-queued">● Queued</span>
                <span class="check-state state-running"><i></i> In progress</span>
                <span class="check-state state-passed">✓ Passed</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="pr-meta">Runner <code>ownci-mybox</code>: your laptop, not a hosted VM.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="terminal" aria-hidden="true">
          <div class="terminal-chrome"><span class="chrome-dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span> ~/code/app</div>
          <pre class="terminal-body"><span class="t-cmd">ownci start --detach</span><span class="t-ok">✓ Runner 'ownci-mybox' is listening for jobs on acme/app (pid 48213). Stop with: ownci stop</span><span class="t-gap"></span><span class="t-note"># PR #42 pushed: GitHub queues "build" for runs-on: [ownci]</span><span class="t-note"># your runner takes the job and starts container node:22</span><span class="t-note"># checkout · npm ci · npm test</span><span class="t-pass"># job succeeded: the check turns green on the PR</span><span class="t-note"># _work wiped in a throwaway container, ready for the next job</span></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="run-caption">Illustration · the first two lines are real OwnCI output</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="split-section" aria-labelledby="problem-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-kicker">The problem</p>
        <h2 id="problem-title">Your CI waits in someone else’s queue while your own machine sits idle.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Most pull request checks run on hosted runners: shared virtual machines that start from a
          clean image every time and, on private repositories, are billed by the minute. Meanwhile
          the laptop or workstation you’re sitting at is usually doing nothing.
        </p>
        <p>
          GitHub already lets you run jobs on your own hardware with <em>self-hosted runners</em>.
          Turning one on is a small project, though: download and verify the runner, register it
          with a token, keep the process alive, clean up between jobs, and remember how to take it
          all out again. And by default a self-hosted runner runs job steps <em>directly on your
          machine</em>, as you.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="pain-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-kicker">What gets in the way</p>
        <h2 id="pain-title">Three reasons people don’t use their own compute.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <article class="card problem">
          <span class="card-number">01</span>
          <h3>Hosted minutes add up</h3>
          <p>On private repositories, hosted runner time is metered. Every push to every pull request spends some.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card problem">
          <span class="card-number">02</span>
          <h3>Self-hosting is a chore</h3>
          <p>Download, checksum, register, supervise, clean up, unregister: by hand, and again on every machine.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card problem">
          <span class="card-number">03</span>
          <h3>A plain runner runs on your host</h3>
          <p>A stock self-hosted runner executes steps directly on your machine and leaves files behind for the next job.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="compare-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-kicker">What OwnCI changes</p>
        <h2 id="compare-title">The official runner, without the busywork.</h2>
        <p>
          OwnCI doesn’t replace GitHub Actions or invent a new CI. It installs, supervises and
          cleans up the official <code>actions/runner</code>, and configures it so jobs run in
          containers.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="compare">
        <div class="compare-col">
          <h3>A runner set up by hand</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Find the right runner release and verify it yourself</li>
            <li>Paste a registration token into <code>config.sh</code></li>
            <li>Keep <code>run.sh</code> alive and restart it after a crash</li>
            <li>Jobs run straight on your host</li>
            <li>One job’s leftovers are still there for the next</li>
            <li>The generic <code>self-hosted</code> label can catch any workflow</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="compare-col ours">
          <h3>With OwnCI</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><code>ownci setup</code> downloads the pinned runner and checks its SHA-256 before registering</li>
            <li>Your GitHub token is never stored, and the registration token is single-use</li>
            <li>A supervisor restarts the runner with backoff; <code>ownci stop</code> stops it cleanly</li>
            <li>Every job runs in a Docker container</li>
            <li>The workspace is wiped after every job, and a failed wipe fails the job visibly</li>
            <li>An opt-in <code>ownci</code> label, so a stray <code>runs-on: self-hosted</code> never lands on your laptop</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="lifecycle-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-kicker">One command each</p>
        <h2 id="lifecycle-title">Set it up, start it, stop it, remove it.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="cmd-strip">
        <div class="cmd"><code>ownci setup</code><p>Download and verify the runner, register it with your repository, install the isolation settings.</p></div>
        <div class="cmd"><code>ownci start</code><p>Re-check isolation, clean up leftovers, and start taking jobs. Add <code>--detach</code> to run in the background.</p></div>
        <div class="cmd"><code>ownci stop</code><p>Stop contributing compute. The registration is kept, so <code>start</code> brings it back.</p></div>
        <div class="cmd"><code>ownci uninstall</code><p>Stop the runner and, if you ask, unregister it on GitHub and remove OwnCI’s files.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="callout"><strong>Not sure your machine is ready?</strong> <code>ownci doctor</code> checks the platform, your user, Docker, ICU and the isolation settings, and <code>ownci status</code> reports what is actually running.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="split-section" aria-labelledby="same-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-kicker">What stays the same</p>
        <h2 id="same-title">Your workflows, your PR checks, two lines different.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          GitHub still owns workflows, queuing, scheduling and the check on the pull request. You
          point a job at your runner with the <code>ownci</code> label and give it a container
          image. That’s the whole change.
        </p>
        <Code label="workflow.yml" code={workflow} />
        <p>
          Workflows without a <code>container:</code> line can run unchanged in <a href={href('/docs/workflows/')}>container-runner mode</a>, and a team can share one workflow
          so everyone’s jobs <a href={href('/docs/workflows/#team')}>run on their own machine</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="honest-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-kicker">Built for code you trust</p>
        <h2 id="honest-title">What OwnCI is, and what it isn’t.</h2>
      </div>
      <ul class="honest-list">
        <li><span class="mark yes" aria-hidden="true">✓</span><span><strong>For private repositories whose workflows you trust</strong>: your own and your team’s.</span></li>
        <li><span class="mark yes" aria-hidden="true">✓</span><span><strong>Containers and a clean workspace for every job</strong>, re-checked every time the runner starts.</span></li>
        <li><span class="mark no" aria-hidden="true">!</span><span><strong>Not a sandbox for hostile code.</strong> The official runner mounts the Docker socket into job containers, so a job can control your Docker daemon. The container guards against accidents, not attacks.</span></li>
        <li><span class="mark no" aria-hidden="true">!</span><span><strong>Public repositories are refused by default</strong>, because pull requests from forks could run code on your machine.</span></li>
        <li><span class="mark no" aria-hidden="true">!</span><span><strong>Early days:</strong> Linux and WSL2 only, one job at a time per runner, and no auto-start on boot yet.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="callout warn"><strong>Read the trust model before you point OwnCI at anything you don’t control.</strong> <a href={href('/security/')}>What OwnCI enforces, and what it doesn’t →</a></p>
    </section>

    <section class="cta-band">
      <span class="cta-spark" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>
      <div><p class="section-kicker">Ready when you are</p><h2>Run your next PR check on your own machine.</h2><p>Install, run <code>ownci doctor</code>, and set up your repository.</p></div>
      <a class="button-primary" href={href('/docs/')}>Get started <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
